unknown command "change-request" for "task-board"
Inspect and repair the Story-scoped Git workspaces that repository-mutating
spawns run in.

Each Story gets one managed worktree under .temp/<STORY-ID>/worktree on a
short-lived task-board/story/<STORY-ID> branch forked from trunk. Trunk is
spawn.worktree_isolation.integration_base_branch when configured, and otherwise
resolves from refs/remotes/origin/HEAD and then the current branch.

These commands require a local authoritative board. On a remote board they
refuse with worktree_isolation_requires_local_board and do nothing else.

Usage:
  task-board worktree [command]

Available Commands:
  abort                 Stop using a Story workspace, preserving its work by default
  checkpoint            Commit an accepted leaf onto its Story branch while preserving integrating
  gc                    Release Story leases whose holding runs are provably terminal
  integrate             Land an accepted Story candidate on trunk and commit its board state
  invalidate-acceptance Release an element whose acceptance §6.2 already invalidated back to rework
  repair                Re-materialize a recorded workspace whose worktree is missing
  status                Report managed Story workspaces and what blocks their cleanup
  transaction           Inspect and dispose of a refused or abandoned integration transaction

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for worktree

Global Flags:
      --board-dir string         Path to the board directory (overrides TASK_BOARD_DIR and config local.board_dir) (default ".task-board")
      --context-profile string   Agent context profile override: full or lite
      --insecure                 Skip TLS certificate verification (for self-signed certs)
      --json                     Output in JSON format
      --no-update-check          Disable automatic update check
      --remote string            Remote board server URL (overrides TASK_BOARD_REMOTE)
      --remote-board string      Remote board ID (overrides TASK_BOARD_ID)

Use "task-board worktree [command] --help" for more information about a command.
